in macroscopic weak tests: expand (a-q)''^j using a''^j=a, and use strong convergence of q with bounded coefficients. The packet model instead has zero odd centered coefficient moments (rotation theta_j -> theta_j+pi/k makes each fixed r_k symmetric); in particular its expected coefficient cube is 0, while p(1-p)(1-2p)>0 on (0,1). For this model the sum of coefficient cubes also converges to zero in probability: nonzero cross-covariances occur only on bounded-ratio resonance lines (k=l, k=3l or l=3k), so their total is O(X), with bounded sixth moments. The formal model therefore satisfies the binary FIRST/TWO moment identities and positivity but fails the full binary law.

PROVED quantitative skew forced by binary profiles. Using the quoted rho=.815276 bound and weighted Jensen,

    integral p(1-p)(1-2p)
